diff --git a/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md b/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md
index e3f64a1cd..6e7a90c9a 100644
--- a/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md
+++ b/docs/ADR018-render-parity.md
@@ -115,6 +115,8 @@ A single, evidence-based map of how far bex actually matches [render.com](https:
 
 **Health paths are tenant paths (w4/m162, docs checked 2026-10-03):** [Render's free-instance docs](https://render.com/docs/free) spin a service up on any incoming HTTP request or WebSocket connection, with `/robots.txt` as the only documented exception. [Render's health-check docs](https://render.com/docs/health-checks) send instance probes to a path the application chooses. Neither documents a platform-reserved `/healthz`. bex's activator and static-server used to answer `/healthz` themselves with an empty 200. Now every path reaches the tenant's suspended, wake, or static-content handling, and both platform pods use TCP readiness instead. The deliberate differences are unchanged: the bounded 503 wake response ([ADR007](ADR007-restart-suspend-and-resume.md)), the static SPA fallback ([ADR029](ADR029-static-sites.md)), and no `robots.txt` exception in bex. This is documentation parity only. No live Render `/healthz` or suspended-static wire response was captured. On bex, live custom-host and paid-maintenance health paths are covered by unit tests, not live probes.
 
+**Edge compression for web services (w8/m83, 2026-10-16):** Render's load balancer compresses app responses with Brotli or gzip ([web services](https://render.com/docs/web-services)); bex served every web response at full size. Each exposed web service's route (`reconcileIngressWithMiddlewares`, so warm serving and the auto-hibernate activator route alike) now carries three App-owned Traefik Middlewares after its access layers and before the WebSocket meter: Traefik's `compress` (`br`, then `gzip`, chosen by the client's `Accept-Encoding` q-values; responses of at least 1 KiB of a listed text, script, style, structured-data or SVG type) bracketed by the `bexCompressGuard` local plugin (`deploy/gitops/charts/traefik-plugins/compressguard`). Measured on the deployed Traefik v3.7.5 (`edge_compression_traefik_test.go`): a request with no offer, `identity` or `q=0` stays identity; an app's own `Content-Encoding` passes through untouched; `206` Range, `HEAD`, WebSocket upgrades, `text/event-stream` and images are not encoded; streamed text is encoded flush by flush. Traefik's `compress` alone would also encode a `Cache-Control: no-transform` response and keep a strong `ETag` on the encoded body (so an `If-Range` resume could splice identity bytes onto a compressed prefix); the guard's inner phase holds `no-transform` responses back and its outer phase weakens a strong `ETag` only on a body the edge encoded, as Render's edge does. HTTP bytes are metered at Traefik's router, after the chain, so bandwidth counts what was actually sent. Static sites keep their own handler compression (ADR029, w8/m79); private services, workers and cron jobs have no public route; a suspended service's 503 page is uncompressed. No API field or setting: REST, GraphQL, MCP, the dashboard and Blueprints are unchanged. Deliberate differences: no zstd (Traefik offers it, Render does not), and the compressed-type list is bex's (`edgeCompressedTypes`), not a vendor's.
+
 **A deploy queued behind a failed build always runs (w6/m100, 2026-08-26):** a live hunt triggered a second deploy while the first was still building, the first failed its build (wrong branch → clone exit 90 → `PodFailurePolicy`), and the second stayed at `status:"queued"` with empty `startedAt`/`finishedAt`/`failureReason` and a frozen `updatedAt` for 185+ seconds — indefinitely, in fact: only a human clicking Cancel closed it. Render's behaviour is the parity target — a deploy queued behind a sibling that fails takes the build slot the failure freed. The cause was generation bookkeeping, not the wire contract: the operator stamped a build failure's durable marker with `metadata.generation`, which the queued deploy's own spec patch had already bumped, so the re-dispatch gate read the first deploy's verdict as the second's and halted before ever building it. The terminal build verdict now rides its own `status.conditions[Build]`, attributed to the release generation that actually built, and both the operator's re-dispatch gate and bex-api's deploy projection read it there. **No wire-shape change:** the eleven-value status vocabulary, `renderDeploy`'s fields, the GraphQL/MCP projections (MCP literally reuses REST's `toRenderDeploy`), and the dashboard's status/label/polling logic are all untouched — this is a liveness fix, so the same row simply now reaches `build_in_progress` → `live`/`build_failed` instead of never leaving `queued`. One consequence worth naming: the deploy whose build failed still closes `build_failed` **with its build error**, not `canceled`. It would have become `canceled` under the plain generation comparison, because the operator adopts the queued generation milliseconds after recording the failure while bex-api resyncs every 30s — the durable condition is what keeps the verdict readable that late. Regression coverage: `build_failed_pending_release_envtest_test.go` (operator), `queued_behind_failure_test.go` (bex-api, including the Rollback call site).
 
 **Deploy-detail timeline/actions (w5/m29 + w2/m38, refreshed 2026-07-15):** the detail page renders exact-deploy row/event facts, shared Cancel/Rollback controls, all eleven status labels/filters, and correct open-versus-terminal polling. The store now persists the evidence-backed lifecycle and `updatedAt`; the timeline never invents a skipped phase. Events links never use the unrelated `evt-…` id. Comparison: [render-artifacts/deploy-detail-page.md](render-artifacts/deploy-detail-page.md).
